Assignment 07 – Fun With Objects

CS 414 — Strategy Pattern in C++ and OCaml
Dale Heck

Overview

This assignment was all about taking the Strategy Pattern — something normally shown in OOP — and rebuilding it in two totally different ways: once in C++ using polymorphism, and once in OCaml using higher-order functions and modules. The idea is basically to show how you can swap algorithms at runtime depending on which “strategy” you plug in.

What’s in this folder

Here are the files I included:

C++

Strategy Pattern.cpp
My full C++ program that uses an abstract SortStrategy class with three strategies (QuickSort, MergeSort, BubbleSort). The SortContext holds a pointer to whichever strategy I want to use and calls it on the same vector of numbers.
I also included timing output to show the different sort speeds.

Assignment 7 Sorting Pattern Output.pdf
This is just the screenshot/output showing that all the C++ strategies work.

OCaml

Sort.ml
This file contains my sorting functions: quicksort, mergesort, and a simple bubble sort. They are normal OCaml functions that take a list and return a sorted list.
I use the higher-order function sort_with so I can swap the sorting strategy by passing in the function I want.

Sort_context.ml
This file shows the “functor version” of the strategy idea. It has:

a module type SORT

concrete modules like Quick

a functor SortContext that takes a sorting module and produces a context that can use that strategy

It basically mimics what the C++ version does, just in the OCaml style.
